  "first_lines": "  Vol. 9(2), pp. 136-142, February, 2015\n  DOI: 10.5897/AJEST2014.1829\n  Article Number: 537A7E349850\n  ISSN 1996-0786\n                                                           African Journal of Environmental Science and\n  Copyright © 2015                                                                          Technology\n  Author(s) retain the copyright of this article\n  http://www.academicjournals.org/AJEST\n\n\n\n\nFull Length Research Paper\n\n Effectiveness of neem, cashew and mango trees in the\n  uptake of heavy metals in mechanic village, Nigeria\n                              Ojekunle, Z. O.*, Ubani, D. R. and Sangowusi, R. O.\n                               Federal University of Agriculture, Alabata, Ogun State, Nigeria.\n                                         Received 20 November, 2014; Accepted 8 January, 2015\n\n    The concentrations of heavy metals were determined from the soil of the mechanic village, in Abeokuta\n    and a control farmland located at Federal University of Agriculture, Abeokuta (FUNAAB). The soil\n    sample collected at the base of different species of tree showed that the heavy metals were below\n    permissible levels (FAO/WHO and EC/CODEX standard) and show no significant difference in the range\n    of mean. Absorption of heavy metals by the bark of the trees in the mechanic village was evident when\n    compared relatively to the presence and uptake of the heavy metals from the soil by tree in the\n    farmland. The mean concentrations of the heavy metals in the soil of the farmland are in this order of\n    magnitude Cd>Cu>Pb, while the mean concentration of the heavy metals in the soil of the mechanic\n    village are in the order of magnitude Pb>Cu>Cd. Lead has the least concentration in the farmland, while\n    in the mechanic village, it is the predominant heavy metal detected which also shows greater significant\n    different at p<0.05 with a value of 24.34 mg/kg indicating area of high mechanic activities. The\n    concentration values of heavy metals in the barks in comparison to the standard shows that the\n    concentration of the heavy metals in those vicinities is within the permissible range for cadmium and\n    copper, while lead present was above the WHO/FAO standard at 0.299 mg/kg and close to the\n    EC/CODEX standard. It can also be concluded that the uptake efficiency of heavy metal under study of\n    the three species are in the order magnitude Mango>Cashew>Neem.\n\n    Key words: Absorption, concentration, farmland, magnitude, phytoremediation.\n\n\nINTRODUCTION\n\nMechanic Villages or workshop engage in the finishing                tion, and contaminant degradation abilities of the entire\nprocesses of oil, paints, fuels and other Heavy metals               plant body (Hinchman et al., 1995). Many species of\nwhich are inevitably discharge into the soil and render the          plants have been successful in absorbing contaminants\nsoil derelict and infertile for live except if proper                such as lead, cadmium, chromium, arsenic, and various\nremediation is done to revert the already damage soil to             radionuclides from soil (Bieby et al., 2011).\nfertility. Phytoremediation takes the advantage of the                  Heavy metals are the most dangerous contaminants\nunique and selective uptake capabilities of plant root               since they are persistent and accumulate in water,\nsystems, together with the translocation, bioaccumula-               sediments and in tissues of the living organisms, through\n\n*Corresponding author. E-mail: oojekunle@yahoo.com, ojekunlezo@funaab.edu.ng.\n\nAuthor(s) agree that this article remain permanently open access under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License 4.0\nInternational License\n\f                                                                                                           Ojekunle et al.      137\n\n\n\n\nFigure 1. Map of Odeda local government, indicating the mechanic village. Source: GIS laboratory, Federal University of Agriculture\nAbeokuta, 2014.\n\n\n\ntwo mechanisms, namely “bioconcentration” (uptake from              carried out an estimation of heavy metals in Neem tree\nthe ambient environment) and “biomagnification” (uptake             leaves along Katsina – Dutsinma – Funtua Highway in\nthrough the food chain) (Chaphekar, 1991). Hyperaccu-               Katsina State of Nigeria. Bieby et al. (2011) conducted a\nmulators are plants that can absorb high levels of                  review on heavy metals (As, Pb, and Hg) uptake by\ncontaminants concentrated either in their roots, shoots             plants through phytoremediation. Raskin et al. (1997)\nand/or leaves (Penkala, 2005). Baker and Brooks have                understudied phytoremediation of metals using plants to\ndefined metal hyperaccumulator as plants that contain               remove pollutants from the environment. Thus\nmore than or up to 0.1% that is more than (1000 mg/g) of            establishing the importance for the study of Neem\ncopper, cadmium, chromium, lead, nickel cobalt or 1% (>             (Azadirachta indica), cashew (Anacardium occidentale),\n10,000 mg/g) of zinc or manganese in the dry matter.                and mango (Manifera indica) trees as possible\n   Various plants have been used as bioindicators to                bioindicators of heavy metals in the environment.\nassess the impact of a pollution source on the vicinity               The mechanic village studied is located along the\nwhich is due to high metal accumulation of plants (Onder            Abeokuta – Ibadan expressway, Odeda local\nand Dursun, 2006). Devendra et al. (2013), have                     government, Ogun state, Nigeria. It covers a large span\ninvestigated in their study of the bioindicators: A                 of land and serves as the major mechanic workshop for",
