  "first_lines": "         Bima Journal of Science and Technology, Vol. 10(1A) Mar, 2026 ISSN: 2536-6041\n\n           E-ISSN: 3115-4662       DOI: 10.64290/bima.v10i1A.1514\n Assessment of Carcinogenic and Non- carcinogenic Health Risks of Metals in the Drinking\n                   Water of Gombe Local Government Area, Nigeria.\n                           Sule S.1*, Olowolafe E. A.2 and Dabi D. D.2\n 1\n  Department of Geography and Environmental Management, Faculty of Sciencex, Gombe State\n                           University, PMB 127 Gombe, Nigeria\n     2\n      Department of Geography and Planning, University of Jos, Jos, Plateau State, Nigeria\n                            Corresponding Author: ssule@gsu.edu.ng\n                                           ABSTRACT\nThe carcinogenic and non-carcinogenic health risks of heavy metals (Pb, Fe, Cu, Cr, Mn, and Cd)\nin drinking water sources in Gombe LGA, Nigeria, were assessed. A total of 87 samples of water\nwere collected from the tap, boreholes, wells, vendor water, rainwater, sachet water, and bottled\nwater in the 11 wards of Gombe LGA. The concentrations of heavy metal were analyzed using\natomic absorption spectrophotometry, while the carcinogenic and non-carcinogenic health risks\nwere determined using Chronic Daily Intake (CDI), Hazard Quotient (HQ), Hazard Index (HI),\nand Incremental Lifetime Cancer Risk (ILCR) models in accordance with USEPA-approved\nequation. The results indicated an elevated non-carcinogenic risks (HI ≥ 4) in tap, well, and sachet\nwater for both infants, children, and adults, with Cr and Pb as the major contributors. Borehole,\nrain, vendor, and bottled water sources were found to be low to medium risk levels. The\ncarcinogenic risk assessment showed that the ILCR values for Cr, Pb, and Cd in tap, well, and\nsachet water sources, exceeded the acceptable threshold (1 × 10⁻⁴), especially for adults and\nchildren, and chromium is the main cause of lifetime cancer. The study came to the conclusion\nthat some drinking water sources in Gombe LGA, especially tap, well, and sachet water, pose\nserious public health risks. Thus, strict regulatory enforcement, better water treatment, and\nongoing monitoring are constantly needed to lower metal contamination and protect vulnerable\ngroups.\nKeywords: Carcinogenic risk, Drinking water, Gombe, Heavy metals, Non-carcinogenic risk.\n             INTRODUCTION                            can affects vulnerable groups, such as\nAccording to the World Health Organization           children, the elderly, and those with\n(WHO), illnesses linked to contaminated              weakened immune systems (Thalia, 2024).\ndrinking water cause millions of deaths              The US Environmental Protection Agency\nannually, making waterborne diseases a               (US EPA, 2020) highlights the potential\nmajor global public health concern (WHO,             health risks associated with chemical\n2019). Access to clean and safe drinking             contamination of groundwater. To evaluate\nwater has a detrimental effect on public             these risks, the cancer risk and non-cancer\nhealth, economic development, and overall            hazard index are calculated for each metal,\nwell-being in many Nigerian communities,             distinguishing between carcinogenic and\nsuch as Gombe Local Government Area                  non-carcinogenic         health      hazards\n(LGA), where waterborne illnesses like               (Weitekamp et al., 2021). Cancer risk refers\ncholera, diarrhea, typhoid fever and cancer          to the likelihood of developing cancer due to\nare common (Nwinyi et al., 2020;                     exposure to chemical contaminants in\nMuhammad, 2022; Umar et al., 2022; Sule              drinking water (US EPA, 2020). According\net al., 2022). The burden of these illnesses         to US EPA guidelines, a cancer risk of 1 in 1\n\n                                                20\n\f         Bima Journal 