  "first_lines": "                                                                                      Majalah Kedokteran Bandung (MKB)\n      RESEARCH ARTICLE\n                                                                                      Received: March 7, 2022\npISSN: 0126-074X | eISSN: 2338-6223                                                   Accepted: May 20, 2022\nhttps://doi.org/10.15395/mkb.v54n3.2729                                               Available online: September 30, 2022\nMajalah Kedokteran Bandung. 2022;54(3):154–160\n\n\n     Antioxidant Activities of Ginger (Zingiber officinale) and Telang Flower\n                     (Clitoria ternatea L.) Combination Tea\n\n   Wahyu Widowati,1 Teresa Liliana Wargasetia,1 Teddy Marcus Zakaria,1 Meganitha,2 Michael Sebastian\n                  Gunadi,³ Nathanael Halim,³ Nindia Salsabila Mia Dewi,4 Sherly Santiadi3\n                    1\n                     Faculty of Medicine, Maranatha Christian University, Bandung, Indonesia\n           2\n             Aretha Medika Utama, Biomolecular and Biomedical Research Center, Bandung, Indonesia\n             3\n              Faculty of Information Technology, Maranatha Christian University, Bandung, Indonesia\n 4\n  Faculty of Mathematics and Natural Sciences Education, Indonesian Education University, Bandung, Indonesia\n\n\n\n\n Abstract\n\n Tea is widely consumed for health purposes to prevent and treat various diseases. This paper reports the\n antioxidant activities of a herbal tea namely jahe (ginger, Zingiber officinale) telang (Clitoria ternatea L.), and\n the combination namely JaTe (jahe and telang). Each of jahe, telang, and JaTe teas were made of 2g ginger, 5\n buds dried telang flower, and the combination respectively. The teas were boiled in 200 mL for 5 minutes. The\n dried ginger, telang, and JaTe formulation were evaluated for antioxidant activities. Total phenolic content was\n evaluated by Folin-Ciocalteu using the gallic acid equivalent (GAE), while total flavonoid content was evaluated\n by aluminum chloride colorimetric assay using the quercetin equivalent (QE). The antioxidant activity was\n measured using 2,2-diphenyl-1picrylhydrazyl (DPPH), and 2,2'-Azino-Bis-3-Ethylbenzothiazoline-6-sulfonic\n acid (ABTS), hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂) scavenging activities, and ferric reducing antioxidant power (FRAP) assay.\n The highest phenolic and flavonoid contents were found in ginger tea with 23.62 μg GAE/100% concentration\n and 7.22 μg QE/100% concentration, followed by telang tea with 16.20 μg GAE/100% concentration and 4.88\n μg QE/100% concentration. Ginger tea has the strongest DPPH scavenging activity with IC₅₀=7.76% followed\n by telang tea 17.07%. JaTe tea has the strongest ABTS scavenging activity with IC₅₀=1.01% followed by ginger\n tea with IC₅₀=1.26%. Ginger tea has the strongest H₂O₂ scavenging activity with IC₅₀=13.23% and followed by\n JaTe tea with IC₅₀=14.66%. JaTe tea has the strongest FRAP activity with IC₅₀=3.59%, followed by ginger tea\n with IC₅₀=4.94%. Overall, JaTe herbal tea formulation has a strong antioxidant activity.\n\n Keywords: Antioxidant, clitoria ternatea, herbal tea, zingiber officinale\n\n\n\n\nIntroduction                                                                     compounds and their antioxidant activity. The\n                                                                                 phenolic content in tea is strictly related to\nTea is known as one of the favorite beverages                                    the processing steps during the production of\nwithin the international, together with coffee                                   different tea. The difference between teas in\nand cocoa. Tea has been used as a health                                         general and herbal teas is that herbal teas are\nproduct or medicinal drug to prevent and deal                                    made from a combination of dry ingredients of\nwith various diseases. Previous studies have                                     leaves, seeds, grass, nuts, bark, fruits, and flowers\nproven the benefits of tea, including antioxidant,                               and that give them the taste of other herbal plants\nbacteriostatic, anticancer, and regulation of lipid                              that have pharmacological activity. The chemical\nmetabolism.1 In Indonesia, the teas that are often                               composition of tea has been thoroughly studied.\nconsumed by the public are green tea, black tea,                                 The main compound from tea leaves has a high\noolong tea, and white tea. Most of the beneficial                                polyphenol content in dry weight.2,3\neffects of tea are mainly to its polyphenolic                                        Ginger (Zingiber officinale) is a known and\n                                                                                 widely used species that is rich in numerous\nCorresponding Author:                                                            chemical compounds, such as phenolic, terpenes,\nWahyu Widowati,                                                                  polysaccharides, lipids, and natural acids. The\nFaculty of Medicine, Maranatha Christian University,\nJalan Surya Sumantri No. 65 Bandung 40164, West Java,                            main compound of ginger that has a health\nIndonesia                                                                        benefit is its phenolic compounds, which include\nEmail: wahyu_w60@yahoo.com\nCopyright @ 2022 by Authors. This is an Open Access article licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 International License (http://",
