  "first_lines": "                  Revista                                                                                                     Editorial\n                                                       2023                                                                 Neogranadina\n             Facultad de                       julio-diciembre\n\n         Ciencias Básicas                        Vol. 19(2) ISSN: 1900-4699 ▪ e-ISSN: 2500-5316               ■   pp. 75-90\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.18359/rfcb.7981\n\n\n\n\n              Potential Applications of Hermetia\n              illucens (Black Soldier Fly Larvae)\n              for Producing Fertilizer (Frass) from\n              Organic Waste Derived from Rum\n              Production (Alcoholic Beverage)*\n              Juan Pablo Ospina-Yepesa ■ Juan Fernando García Trejob ■ Nelson Ceballos-\n              Aguirrec ■ Alejandro Hurtado Salazard\n\n                  Abstract: The study examines the performance of Black Soldier Fly larvae (Hermetia illucens) on or-\n                  ganic waste generated by the Caldas Liquor Industry (ILC). Three phases were designed to evaluate\n                  larval performance on this waste. Phase I: involved behavioral tests conducted through a bioassay,\n                  assessing substrate doses of 50, 75, 100, 125, 150, and 175 grams. On average, the larvae consumed\n                  1.1 g of waste, suggesting that approximately 90,909 larvae are required to process 100 kilograms.\n                  The 75 g and 125 g treatments yielded the best results, while doses below 75 g caused larval starva-\n                  tion. Phase II: focused on analyzing the frass substrate for heavy metals, revealing low concentra-\n                  tions of arsenic, nickel, and mercury. Microbiological analyses detected no Salmonella spp. or E. coli.\n                  The chemical composition of the frass indicated potential for use as a fertilizer and as an alternative\n                  to chemically synthesized fertilizers. Phase III: evaluated the phytotoxicity of frass produced from\n                  coffee pulp and residues derived from rum production on the germination of tomato, onion, and\n                  cucumber seeds. The study aims to identify the potential of these organic residues for fertilization\n                  and their viability as alternative fertilizer sources.\n\n                  Keywords: Phytotoxicity; Hermetia Illucens; Behavioral Tests; Liquor Production Residues; Frass\n                  Organic Substrate\n\n\n              *   Research Article\n              a   Master's Degree in Advanced Technologies for Agroforestry Development. Professor. Universidad de\n                  Caldas, Manizales, Colombia.\n                  Email:juan.ospina@ucaldas.edu.co; ORCID: https://orcid.org/0009-0001-4795-4237\n              b   Doctor. Professor of Biosystems Engineering. Universidad Autónoma de Querétaro, Quetétano, México.\n                  Email: fernando.garcia@uaq.mx; ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3372-0878\n              c   Doctor. Professor of Agricultural Sciences. Universidad de Caldas, Manizales, Colombia.\n                  Email: nelson.ceballos@ucaldas.edu.co; ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8463-3379\n              d   Doctor of Science in Plant Science.Universidad de Caldas, Manizales, Colombia. Email:\n                  alejandro.salazar@ucaldas.edu.co; ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1251-125X\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                                                   75\n\f       Recibido: 26/05/2025       Aceptado: :10/11/2025         Disponible en línea: 30/12/2025\n       Cómo citar: J. P. Ospina Yepes, N. Ceballos Aguirre, J. F. García Trejo, y A. Hurtado Salazar, «Potential\n       Applications of Hermetia illucens (Black Soldier Fly Larvae) for Producing Fertilizer (Frass) from Orga-\n       nic Waste Derived from Rum Production (Alcoholic Beverage)», Rev. Fac. Cienc. Básicas, vol. 19, n.º 2,\n       pp. 75–90, dic. 2025.\n\n\n     Potenciales aplicaciones de las larvas de mosca Soldado\n     Negra (Hermetia Illucens) para la fabricación de fertilizantes\n     (excrementos) a partir de residuos orgánicos provenientes de la\n     elaboración de bebidas alcohólicas (Ron)\n       Resumen: El estudio se centra en el rendimiento de las larvas de la mosca soldado negra (Hermetia\n       illucens) en residuos orgánicos de la Industria Licorera de Caldas (ILC). Se establecieron tres fases\n       para evaluar el rendimiento de las larvas en los desechos. En la Fase I, se realizaron pruebas de\n       comportamiento utilizando un bioensayo, evaluando dosis de 50, 75, 100, 125, 150 y 175 gramos.\n       Las larvas consumieron un promedio de 1.1 g de los desechos, lo que indica que se requieren 90,909\n       larvas para 100 kilogramos. Los tratamientos de 75 y 125 gramos mostraron la mejor respuesta,\n       mientras que menos de 75 gramos causaron que las larvas murieran de hambre. En la Fase II, se",
