ination for FAs metabolism, but the most\nsignificant effect was confirmed for plant growing period.\n\nKeywords: abiotic stress; heavy metals; peroxidation of lipids; Spinacia oleracea L.\n\n\n  Cadmium (Cd) is a heavy metal released into the           interaction with the antioxidant defense system,\nenvironment by thermal power and heating plants,            disruption of the electron transport chain or in-\nmetal industries, urban traffic, sewage sludge              duction of lipid peroxidation (Cuypers et al. 2010).\nand phosphate fertilizers (Pavlíková et al. 2002a,          It decreases water stress tolerance of plants. The\nVollmann et al. 2015). Plants have no metabolic             accumulation of Cd in plant tissues caused dam-\nrequirement for Cd, however it is relatively easy           ages to the photosynthetic apparatus; it inhibited\navailable to plants. A frequent outcome following           photosynthesis by increasing stomatal and meso-\nexposure to Cd is the overproduction of reactive            phyll resistance to carbon dioxine uptake (Gallego\noxygen species, potentially causing oxidative dam-          et al. 2012). The reduction in photosynthetic rate\nage in plant cells and thus requiring the interven-         led to a limited supply of metabolic energy and\ntion of antioxidant defense systems (Sandalio et            therefore to nitrogen (N) assimilation restriction.\nal. 2001). Cadmium induces oxidative stress in              Nitrogen flow through amino acids can change in\nplants by blocking essential functional groups in           response to Cd stress. Plants that were exposed\nbiomolecules and by indirect mechanisms such as             to toxic elements have also been shown to ac-\nSupported by the Czech University of Life Sciences Prague, Project No. CIGA 20142004, and by the Institute of Organic\nChemistry and Biochemistry, Project No. RVO: 61388963.\n\n                                                                                 